your papers please says igor mangushev  he and his men from a self-appointed  militia group sift through a dilapidated  house in the middle of moscow  without any legal authority migrants  from central asia are thought to be  hiding here  they are a source of cheap labor often  without valid  we are from the presnya district militia  keep your documents ready and wait for  the police  mangushev and his men do this week after  week  residents tip them off as to where  migrants could be living  many think immigrants are a problem for  moscow  my papers are in order this man tells me  he's mistaken  for a member of the militia  i ask him where he's from  uzbekistan he says you came here for  work i ask  yes he says here in moscow there are  jobs  at home it's difficult  now he is desperate mangushev has called  the police  and that means the man from uzbekistan  will probably be deported  this has nothing to do with nationalism  says  it's about different civilizations  central asia is a region where  fundamentally different rules apply than  in moscow  they don't make the slightest effort to  accept our russian rules or to integrate  here  a survey by the last remaining  independent russian polling organization  shows that many people in moscow think  like this  about 80 percent of muscovites want the  immigrants from central asia to be sent  home  but it would be hard to imagine moscow's  construction boom without them  on many of the building sites it's  migrants who do the hard labor  and at this construction site on the  outskirts of the russian capital  they have now gone on strike  from tajikistan and his team have gone  more than three months without pay  they are owed a total of more than 100  000 euros  but the man from the construction  company dimitri yarosh  says he knows nothing about it there's  probably not much morado and his workers  from tajikistan can do  they can't bring those responsible to  account  that really shouldn't be happening we'll  have to clarify that with our  subcontractors  who hired these people as a general  contractor we do not employ any  construction workers we hire a company  it pays its people and it must also  provide accommodation for them  the tajik workers live in containers  directly on the construction site  and mouradov says they have to pay for  it the equivalent of around 70 euros per  month  he wants to show me how they live and  leads me around the grounds  cooking eating sleeping they have  everything they need  in the containers  some workers even live here with their  families and small children  none of them could afford to rent a real  apartment they only earn about  800 euros a month if they get paid at  all  times have got tougher muradov says  what do they still need us for i've now  been here in russia for over 10 years  and we've built a lot everywhere you  look now perhaps it's  enough for them maybe they now want to  chase us away  hundreds of thousands of migrant workers  live in moscow  some estimate there are about two  million they work in construction  as cleaners or nannies as taxi drivers  or street sweepers  and many are traders in the big outdoor  markets  when they see that i'm filming here many  become nervous  time and again nationalists have  attacked markets like this  now the mood has changed even among  ordinary muscovites  hostility towards those who do not look  russian is growing  the citizens aid organization has been  observing this for a long time  irina elena and her colleagues provide  advice and medical help to migrants and  refugees  this young man from uzbekistan was  brutally attacked and robbed by a group  of russians  he was about to transfer money to his  family at home  money he earns as a tailor in moscow  this activist translates the young man's  story  he says the young man was tied up and  driven to a forest where he was beaten  he feels migrants can be often attacked  with impunity in russia nowadays  the media are now constantly he says  almost hysterically reporting about  immigrants  this probably influences people and that  leads to this kind of harassment  this is probably the main reason  irena gets 30 to 40 calls for help every  day  the aggression migrants face is greater  than ever  the social worker and human rights  activist says the hatred is directed  toward  all migrants sometimes it's against  caucasians from chechnya or dagestan  people who are russian citizens  there's also hatred targeted at refugees  from far away countries who look  different  like people from africa who've come to  seek asylum  and it's aimed at economic migrants  she shows me videos on the internet to  help me understand  why the atmosphere has now become so  charged  the pictures she shows me are from  mid-october  in southern moscow an angry mob storms a  market  the workers here come mainly from the  caucasus and central asia  a few days earlier a young muscovite had  been stabbed to death in broad daylight  there were reports a man who looked like  he was from the caucasus was to blame  and there were rumors he was hiding at  the market  the police were slow to stop the rioters  but the next day they arrested about a  thousand migrants  to prevent further violence the  authorities said  irina says that's always the way  she says the government encourages  migrants  it recently came up with an immigration  policy in black and white it said russia  needs  labor migrants in this country the birth  rate is catastrophically low she says  it'll need plenty of manpower in the  future  i am surprised that russia has gone so  long without a clear  immigration policy immigrants from the  former soviet republics in central asia  can come to russia without a visa  and stay for three months but many stay  longer and work  illegally at their own risk or because  their employer bribes the authorities  migrant workers are used to having no  rights  authorities say there are more than  three and a half million  undocumented immigrants in russia the  real numbers are probably  much higher whenever public anger  reaches boiling point  they are the scapegoats and then there  are official raids  inspections and waves of deportations  but usually nothing happens mangushev  says  that was why he and his friends decided  to take the fight against illegal  immigration into their own hands  they've been doing this for the past two  years they have carried out more than  100 raids and filmed  everything igor manguchev shows me  pictures of their last major action  they tracked down several dozen  immigrants and handed them over to the  authorities  they often force their way into the  hiding places  go go get your documents out igor  manguchev calls out in this video  all this is completely legal he says  after all they call the police as soon  as they find  migrant workers here they discovered 45  immigrants  and illegal drugs and numerous  electronics  drug trafficking and petty crime these  are the usual accusations against  immigrants  has called in the special police forces  for help  wearing helmets and bulletproof vests  they lead away the defenseless migrants  thinks he did the right thing  he says there are only two possibilities  either the problem will be solved from  the ground up with his group's help  or it will be the end of the political  system the disintegration of russia  religious and national conflicts have  long reached the boiling point he says  goes back into the night with his  militia  the police were suspicious of them at  first he says  many officials profited from immigrants  fears  they would provide cover for the  illegals in exchange for a fat bribe  but now many police officers are working  together with the vigilantes  they can't stop immigration but  igor mangushev and his men are  succeeding  in stirring up xenophobia and conflict  in russia
 

